import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `Show` - Implementation-Storage.md part 7's instances.
   ==========================================================================================

   The bounds below are the table that document gives, and each is exact rather than generous: the
   buffer is sized from them, so a bound larger than the truth wastes the difference and a bound
   smaller than the truth is the one thing an instance may not do.
-}

-- `"False"` is five units, which is the longer of the two.
instance Show(Bool):
    fn show(value: Bool, &to: String) -> {}:
        if value then String.push(to, "True")
        else String.push(to, "False")

    fn showBound(value: Bool) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(5)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The integers, and the digit machinery every decimal in this library is written through.
   ==========================================================================================

   `pushUnsigned` is the whole implementation: a magnitude in decimal, appended. `showSigned` below
   it is a sign and a call, and `Float/Show.yana`'s digit forms are the same loop with a count given
   rather than discovered. There is one decimal-digit loop in Core and this is it.

   **Two digits at a time, out of a two hundred unit table.** `value / 100` and the remainder as an
   index is one division, one multiply and one pair of loads per *two* digits, against a division and
   a remainder per digit - which is what this was. The table is the classic one and its whole content
   is in its name: unit `2n` and `2n + 1` are the tens and the units of `n`.

   **The digits go into a `[CodeUnit *20]` and reach the sink in one block copy**, with no temporary
   string anywhere. The digits come out least-significant first and a sink can only be appended to,
   so the obvious form pushes them into a *heap* string and reads them back out backwards - one
   allocation, one free and one bounds-checked load per digit, per integer formatted. A fixed array
   is written from its end downwards, which puts them in order with no reversal at all, and
   `String.appendUnits` is the one copy that follows. Twenty units is exact: `18446744073709551615` is the
   widest magnitude a `U64` has and it is twenty digits.

   Worth 4.2x on the digit generation and 3.1x on `show(Int)` including the sink's own allocation -
   `test/bench/numbers/` is the harness and §62 of test/bench/findings.md is the measurement.

   **Not vectorized, and that is a measurement rather than an omission** - §62.2. Eight digits at a
   time is expressible over `Vec(U32, 4)` and it loses: the value arrives in a register and a lane
   has to be *inserted* into a vector one at a time, which costs more than the four table iterations
   it replaces at every width this loop actually sees. What a vector is for is the reading direction,
   where the digits arrive in memory sixteen at a time already - see `Core/Read.yana`.
-}
let decimalPairs = "00010203040506070809101112131415161718192021222324252627282930313233343536373839404142434445464748495051525354555657585960616263646566676869707172737475767778798081828384858687888990919293949596979899"

{-
   A magnitude in decimal, appended - the native body, which is the loop above.

   `at` walks down from the end of the buffer and is where the answer starts; the two-digit loop
   stops with one or two digits left, which is the one place the count is odd and is why the tail is
   written out rather than folded into the loop.
-}
@platform(native) fn pushUnsigned(value: U64, &to: String) -> {}:
    let &buffer = [0] :: [CodeUnit *20]

    let &at = 20 :: Size
    let &rest = value

    while rest >= 100:
        let quotient = rest / 100
        let pair = truncate((rest - quotient * 100) * 2) :: Size

        at = at - 2
        buffer[at] = truncate(String.unit(decimalPairs, pair)) :: CodeUnit
        buffer[at + 1] = truncate(String.unit(decimalPairs, pair + 1)) :: CodeUnit

        rest = quotient

    if rest >= 10:
        let pair = truncate(rest * 2) :: Size
        at = at - 2
        buffer[at] = truncate(String.unit(decimalPairs, pair)) :: CodeUnit
        buffer[at + 1] = truncate(String.unit(decimalPairs, pair + 1)) :: CodeUnit
    else:
        at = at - 1
        buffer[at] = truncate(48 + rest) :: CodeUnit

    String.appendUnits(to, values(buffer) + at, 20 - at)

{-
   And the host's own, which is one call.

   `String(x)` on a `bigint` is its decimal and on a `number` an integer's decimal, so the loop above
   has no reason to exist here - and the one it replaced was the worst code in this file on that
   target, since `String.pushUnit` is a *concatenation* there and the reversal spent two of them per digit.
   A fixed array is native-only anyway (Implementation-Containers.md §6), so this was never going to
   be one body.
-}
@platform(js) fn pushUnsigned(value: U64, &to: String) -> {} = String.push(to, hostToString(value))

{-
   A signed value in decimal - the sign, then the magnitude.

   **The magnitude is taken in the unsigned domain**, which is what replaced producing the digits
   downwards from zero. Both are answers to the same problem - the most negative value of any width
   has no positive counterpart, so negating it in its own type gives that same number back - and the
   unsigned negation of a two's complement pattern is the magnitude for every input including that
   one, `9223372036854775808` for `-9223372036854775808`. It costs one instruction and it lets the
   digit loop be written once, over an unsigned value, for every caller in Core.

   Not `pub`: it is how the `Show` instances below are written and not something to call directly,
   since what a program wants of an integer is `show` and the instance decides the rest.
-}
fn showSigned(value: Long, &to: String) -> {}:
    if value < 0:
        String.pushUnit(to, 45)
        pushUnsigned((0 :: U64) - (bitcast(value) :: U64), to)
    else:
        pushUnsigned(bitcast(value) :: U64, to)

{-
   The integers, which is the rest of the ladder.

   `Int`, `Long`, `Size` and `USize` are the four a program that says nothing about widths ends up
   holding - and the first two are `I32` and `I64` below, since Analysis-Spellings.md item 7 made
   one primitive of each width. Everything else here is a width a program *asked* for - a byte read
   out of a buffer, a `U32` hash, an `I16` field of a packed record - and it needs an instance for
   the same reason they do: `U8` and `Int` are one `::` apart, so the type a value already has would
   otherwise decide whether it could be printed at all.

   **Two generic bodies rather than ten**, over the conversion that is already required to exist. The
   ladder generates a `Widen` rung from every integer to the widest of its own sign, so `Widen(a,
   Long)` and `Widen(a, U64)` are exactly the constraints "signed" and "unsigned" spell here, and the
   instance below each one is a call. There is still one decimal-digit loop in this file.

   The two widest are the exception, and it falls out of that spelling rather than being a case: a
   type does not widen into itself, so `I64` and `U64` have no rung to the constraint their own
   bodies would ask for. Each calls the loop directly instead.

   **The `js` bodies are not a repetition of the native ones.** Everything narrower than 64 bits is
   a `number` on that target and `I64`/`U64` are `bigint`, so widening first would put a real
   `BigInt()` in front of a call whose whole body is one `String()`. `hostToString` is generic, so
   the host body is one line for all ten rather than one per width. The constraint is carried on the
   `js` declaration too, unused, so that the two `@platform` bodies have the one signature.
-}
@platform(native) fn (Widen(a, Long)) showFromSigned(value: a, &to: String) -> {} =
    showSigned(widen(value) :: Long, to)

@platform(js) fn (Widen(a, Long)) showFromSigned(value: a, &to: String) -> {} =
    String.push(to, hostToString(value))

@platform(native) fn (Widen(a, U64)) showFromUnsigned(value: a, &to: String) -> {} =
    pushUnsigned(widen(value) :: U64, to)

@platform(js) fn (Widen(a, U64)) showFromUnsigned(value: a, &to: String) -> {} =
    String.push(to, hostToString(value))

{-
   Every bound is the widest that type's decimal gets, counted rather than rounded up - the rule the
   head of this file states, since the buffer is sized from it.

   The signed ones spend a unit on the sign because their most negative value needs it, and it is
   that value rather than the largest positive one that is widest: `-128` is four units where `127`
   is three. `CodeUnit` is at most sixteen bits and unsigned (`IntType::kCodeUnitMaxBits`), so it
   costs what a `U16` costs on every target, and a narrower one spends a bound it did not need rather
   than a truncation it cannot detect - `Size`'s bargain exactly. `WideInt` is 53 bits signed, whose
   magnitude is sixteen digits either way the last bit is read, so seventeen covers the sign.
-}
instance Show(I8):
    fn show(value: I8, &to: String) -> {} = showFromSigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: I8) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(4)

instance Show(U8):
    fn show(value: U8, &to: String) -> {} = showFromUnsigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: U8) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(3)

instance Show(I16):
    fn show(value: I16, &to: String) -> {} = showFromSigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: I16) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(6)

instance Show(U16):
    fn show(value: U16, &to: String) -> {} = showFromUnsigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: U16) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(5)

instance Show(I32):
    fn show(value: I32, &to: String) -> {} = showFromSigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: I32) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(11)

instance Show(U32):
    fn show(value: U32, &to: String) -> {} = showFromUnsigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: U32) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(10)

-- `-9223372036854775808` is twenty units.
instance Show(I64):
    fn show(value: I64, &to: String) -> {} = showSigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: I64) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(20)

instance Show(U64):
    fn show(value: U64, &to: String) -> {} = pushUnsigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: U64) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(20)

{-
   And the three whose width the target answers - Analysis-Modules.md Move 2.

   They go through the generic body of their own sign like every fixed width above, which is what
   makes the target's choice cost nothing: a `Size` is an `Int` on JS and reaches `hostToString` as
   the `number` it already is, rather than being widened into the `bigint` a `Long` is there.

   `Size` and `USize` are the two numbers a program that says nothing about widths still ends up
   holding - `length` answers one and every byte count is the other - so an assertion runs into these
   first, `checkEqual(length(out), 6)` being the most ordinary claim a test makes.

   The bound is the widest end of the type's *bound* rather than the width this target landed on:
   `Size` reaches `-9223372036854775808` and `USize` `18446744073709551615`, both twenty units. A
   narrower target spends a bound it did not need, which is the trade the head of this block states.
-}
instance Show(Size):
    fn show(value: Size, &to: String) -> {} = showFromSigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: Size) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(20)

instance Show(USize):
    fn show(value: USize, &to: String) -> {} = showFromUnsigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: USize) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(20)

instance Show(CodeUnit):
    fn show(value: CodeUnit, &to: String) -> {} = showFromUnsigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: CodeUnit) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(5)

instance Show(WideInt):
    fn show(value: WideInt, &to: String) -> {} = showFromSigned(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: WideInt) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(17)

{-
   A string shows as itself, and its bound is a *runtime* value - which is the row that makes the
   point about `showBound`'s constant-ness not being in the type.

   `Just(11)` for `Int` folds to a literal at a concrete call site and this does not, and neither
   instance had to say which it was. That is the whole of what the `Maybe(Int)` shape buys, and it is
   why a format containing one string and three integers still sizes its buffer in one addition.
-}
instance Show(String):
    fn show(value: String, &to: String) -> {} = String.push(to, value)
    fn showBound(value: String) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(length(value))

{-
   `Maybe(a)` - Implementation-Storage.md part 7's row that shows the bound *composing*.

   The first instance here that is generic and constrained on its own element's, which is the shape
   every *container's* `Show` has: the body dispatches `show` at `a` without knowing what `a` is, and
   the bound below it dispatches `showBound` the same way. Nothing about the two is specific to an
   option, which is why this one is worth having beyond the type it is for.

   **The bound is where the composition is visible.** `Nothing` is seven units. `Just(x)` is six -
   `Just(` and `)` - plus whatever `x` costs, and `x`'s cost is an answer of the same shape: a
   `Just` around a bounded type stays exactly bounded, and one around an unbounded type stays
   unbounded, because `Nothing` propagates through the `match` rather than being replaced by a guess.
   That is the whole of what the `Maybe(Size)` return type buys, and it is why a format holding an
   `Maybe(Int)` and three integers still sizes its buffer in one addition.

   It also depends on a `&` parameter of a constrained generic instance method arriving as a place,
   which is what lets every write to the sink - `String.push(to, "Nothing")` included - typecheck. The
   instance below is the proof for the callee's side; a `fn (Show(a)) shown(value: a) -> Int`
   formatting into a local sink is the proof for the caller's, and `Show.yana` in the test corpus is
   where it is written.
-}
instance (Show(a)) Show(Maybe(a)):
    fn show(value: Maybe(a), &to: String) -> {} = match value:
        Just(inner) -> to ++= "Just({inner})"
        Nothing -> String.push(to, "Nothing")

    -- `Nothing` in, `Nothing` out: an element that cannot say what it costs makes the option that
    -- holds it unable to either, and answering a number here would be the one thing an instance may
    -- not do. The six is `Just(` and `)`, which is the only text this adds.
    fn showBound(value: Maybe(a)) -> Maybe(Size) = match value:
        Just(inner) -> match showBound(inner):
            Just(units) -> Just(units + 6)
            Nothing -> Nothing

        Nothing -> Just(7)
